🔒 1. Closed State (Normal)
✅ Everything is working fine.

All requests are allowed to pass through.

The Circuit Breaker monitors how many are failing.

If the failure rate (like 50%) is crossed in a given number of calls, it opens.

🧠 Think of this like the switch is ON — power (requests) is flowing.

❌ 2. Open State (Broken / Blocked)
🚫 Something is wrong. Stop sending requests.

Requests are immediately blocked.

The fallback method is called instead.

This state remains for waitDurationInOpenState (e.g., 5 seconds).

🧠 Like a switch is OFF — power (requests) is cut off to protect the system.

🔄 3. Half-Open State (Testing)
🤔 Let’s test if the service is back.

It allows only a few test requests.

If they succeed, the circuit goes back to Closed (normal).

If they fail, it goes back to Open again.

🧠 Like someone flipping the switch ON to check if it works — if it’s safe, it stays ON.

🎯 Example
Imagine calling a service 10 times:

If 6 out of 10 fail (60% failure), and your threshold is 50% ➡️ circuit goes Open.

After 5 seconds (waitDurationInOpenState), it goes Half-Open and allows 3 test calls.

If all 3 calls succeed ➡️ circuit goes back to Closed.

If even 1 fails ➡️ it goes back to Open again.

